    concept: `Economic curtailment occurs when the grid operator or developer reduces or stops generation for economic reasons — typically because real-time prices are so low that generation is uneconomic. In a VPPA, the question is: if the project curtails economically, does the buyer receive "deemed generation" (the MWh they would have received at contract price) or only the MWh actually generated?`,
    whyMatters: `Economic curtailment is increasingly common as renewable penetration grows. A project in ERCOT may curtail 5–15% of potential generation per year for economic reasons. If the developer can curtail without deemed generation, the buyer's renewable energy volume falls short of targets and the financial hedge is less effective. Conversely, if buyer bears curtailment, the buyer's energy procurement position can be significantly under-hedged.`,
