    concept: `Force majeure excuses performance obligations when specified events beyond a party's control occur — hurricanes, acts of war, pandemic, grid failure. The key commercial questions: how broadly is FM defined, does FM excuse the payment obligation (or only performance), and how long can FM persist before either party can terminate?`,
    whyMatters: `Broad FM definitions are effectively "get out of jail free" cards for the developer. A developer who has a poorly performing project can invoke a broadly drafted FM clause to excuse delivery shortfalls. The distinction between FM excusing performance (delivery of MWh) vs. FM excusing payment (settlement obligation) is critical — no FM clause should excuse a party from paying money already owed.`,
