    title: 'Change in Law',
    concept: `Change in Law provisions determine what happens when a new law, regulation, tax, or grid rule materially changes the economics of the contract after execution. The three allocations: buyer bears the change (most common for certain tax changes), seller bears it (developer absorbs policy risk), or shared through automatic price adjustment or renegotiation.`,
    whyMatters: `In a 15–20 year contract, regulatory change is nearly certain. FERC market redesigns, state renewable portfolio standard changes, interconnection rule modifications, and carbon pricing all affect VPPA economics. Who bears this risk determines whether the contract is durable through policy shifts. Corporate buyers should push to share regulatory risk rather than absorb it unilaterally.`,
